# The backplane computer (hw/design.md), as far as the emulator models it:
# the CPU and north bridge card, 1 MB of RAM on the backplane, and the
# flash. Step 1 of the emulator plan; the cards come later.
#
# The north bridge maps the CPU's 64 KB through four 16 KB windows. At reset
# W0-W2 are RAM blocks 0-2 and W3 is the top 16 KB of ROM A, read-only, so
# the BIOS starts in flash and shadows itself into bank F (hw/design.md,
# "Boot"). Its registers are at $C680. Add `bootsel` to start with W3 on RAM
# block 63 instead, as when the bus spy has loaded RAM. It is revision 3
# (ID $D3; hw/future/north-bridge-rev3.md): revision 2's WIN0H-WIN3H and VECH
# (A20-A23) and MAP (AP8 sends bank 8 to the video aperture), and the
# timebase (the 1 kHz tick, its interrupt as module D, the millisecond
# counter) and the random number chip. OODOS 0.16 needs it. `revision=2` and
# `revision=1` (B1's, no WINnH, VECH or MAP) are the older bridges, for the
# ROM's tests. The bridge owns the DS3231 real-time clock (ds3231.h), which
# starts at the host's local time.
#
# The bus is the new one (hw/design.md, "The new bus"): 24-bit physical
# addresses, and memory chosen by address, not by module ID. The RAM card
# is 000000-0FFFFF (RAM A-D its quarters); the flash is ROM A at F00000-
# F7FFFF (on the CPU card) and ROM B, the cartridge, at F80000-FFFFFF; a
# window's ROM block b is F00000 + b x $4000. Memory cycles carry the
# master's ID on PS0-PS3 (D for the CPU), so IDs 0-5 and F, once RAM A-D
# and the flash, are empty slots, and the memory lines carry no module=.
# Wait states come only from a card pulling /WAIT (a line's wait=; none here
# needs any): the north bridge's WAIT0-3 slow nothing (step B2).
#
# The flash image is the whole 512 KB ROM A (as a programmer would write
# it), with the BIOS in its top 16 KB: block 31, $F7C000-$F7FFFF. The CPU
# sees $C800-$FFFF of it; $C000-$C7FF is the I/O hole. ROM A and ROM B
# are each two chips, even and odd bytes (the `flash` lines, flash.h). A
# window with RO clear sends a write to the chip of its parity, which takes
# it as part of a command sequence (program a byte, erase a sector or the
# chip, its software ID) and is busy a while after, polled by its status.
#
# ROM A's blocks 0-5 hold the owner's Apple IIe ROMs (hw/design.md, "ROM
# A's layout"), erased in bios.rom: `emulator --apple-rom [N=]FILE` (16 KB,
# or CD,EF) writes image N (0-3, 0 if not given) at N x $4000, and
# `--apple-chars [N=]FILE` its character set at $10000 + N x $2000. The
# BIOS's C806R copies the first good image into bank E before entering
# Apple II mode. ROM B is the game cartridge, erased.
#
# The DMA controller (module 8, $C400), revision 2 (ID $82; hw/design.md,
# "The DMA controller, revision 2"), takes the bus from the CPU for its
# transfers; CONTROL starts at $70, so software turns it on. Its channels
# share one register window, which CONTROL's SELECT points at. A card whose
# line says `drq` pulls the shared /DREQ; the controller polls the cards
# and grants the channel whose CARD names the requesting card's module.
# CARD's reset values give today's wiring: channel 0 module 7 (mass
# storage), channel 1 module 9 (video), channel 2 module A (sound), and
# channel 3 none (memory to memory). The USB card isn't wired to /DREQ.
#
# The Console card (module E, $C700) is the front panel: switches, lights,
# HALT/CONT and a breakpoint on any bus cycle. Without a panel attached (the
# terminal emulator has none) programs can still use its breakpoint and
# HALT, which stop at the debugger.
#
# The video card (module 9, $C480) draws VGA 640x480 (video_card.h). It
# powers up with the display off: software sets the palette, a layer and
# CTRL. `emulator --screen FILE` saves its last frame. Revision 1 (rev=1,
# today's BIOS and OODOS) draws from banks 8-B, which it snoops. Revision 2
# (hw/video-rev2.md, proposed: rev=2, or MachineOptions::video_revision)
# draws from 2 MB of its own memory, reached through the aperture line,
# E00000-E0FFFF, and through its data port, served by DMA channel 1. While
# the north bridge's AP8 (MAP, $C6A5 bit 0) is set, as it is from any reset,
# the CPU card sends bank 8 (080000-08FFFF) to the aperture, from the CPU's
# windows and DMA alike, so old software finds the card where it always
# has; banks 9-B stay RAM. With AP8 clear bank 8 is RAM, and the card is
# reached at E00000 (a window's WINnH $0E). The BIOS clears AP8 when there
# is no revision 2 card. On revision 1 the aperture line is inert and the
# card never asks for DMA.
#
# The USB card (module B, $C580) is the keyboard, mouse and game port
# (usb_card.h). Nothing types on it in the terminal app; the WebUI does.
#
# The sound card (module A, $C500) holds two SIDs and 64 KB for a 6510 of
# its own (sound_card.h): so far its registers, RAM and mailbox. Its DMA
# request (DRQSEL's register, RAMDATA at reset) is served by channel 2.
#
# The mass storage card (module 7, $C380) is an SD card behind an RP2350
# (sd_card.h): sectors through DATA, by CPU or DMA (channel 0). `emulator --sd
# FILE` puts a card image in its slot, written through.
#
# The serial card (module 6, $C300): its TL16C552A isn't modelled yet, so
# the terminal is a STAND-IN, a 6551 at $C300-$C303 (mirrored through the
# block, as before: the BIOS and OODOS use $C300-$C303 only). The card's
# W65C22S VIA is real (via6522.h), at $C340-$C34F: the later line answers
# there, over the 6551's mirror. Both pull IRQL bit 6 (module 6). The
# card's INTSTAT ($C322) isn't modelled: $C322 is still the 6551's mirror
# (its command register), so a handler tells the VIA by its IFR. The VIA's
# ports are the two joysticks (joystick_ports.h): joystick 1 on PA0-PA4,
# joystick 2 on PB0-PB4, up/down/left/right/fire, 0 when pressed; the
# rest pulled up, so `LDA $C341` `AND #$1F` reads $1F with nothing pressed.
# The WebUI drives them from gamepads or its keys; the terminal app's
# debugger has `joy`.
#
#   emulator --machine machines/backplane.machine bios.rom

cpu          w65c02s
northbridge  backplane

# The RAM card's quarters, RAM A-D, 256 KB each, at 000000-0FFFFF, chosen
# by address. The flash lines are offsets into it (space=flash): ROM A at
# memory F00000-F7FFFF, on the CPU card, and ROM B at F80000-FFFFFF.
ram   00000-3FFFF  name=ram_a
ram   40000-7FFFF  name=ram_b
ram   80000-BFFFF  name=ram_c
ram   C0000-FFFFF  name=ram_d
flash 00000-7FFFF  space=flash  name=rom_a  bios.rom
flash 80000-FFFFF  space=flash  name=rom_b  -   # the game cartridge: erased

# The VIA's line must come after the 6551's: the later mapping answers
# (test_joyports checks it).
acia6551  300-37F  space=io  name=terminal  irq     # stand-in serial card
via6522   340-34F  space=io  name=via  irq          # the serial card's VIA, over the 6551's mirror
joyports  name=joysticks  via=via                    # on PA0-4 and PB0-4
sd        380-3FF  space=io  name=sd  irq  drq
dma       400-47F  space=io  name=dma  irq
video     480-4FF  space=io  name=video  irq  drq  rev=2
aperture  E00000-E0FFFF  name=aperture   # revision 2: bank 8 is sent here while AP8 is set
sound     500-57F  space=io  name=sound  irq  drq
usb       580-5FF  space=io  name=usb  irq
disk2     600-67F  space=io  name=disk2               # slot 6 in Apple II mode
console   700-77F  space=io  name=console  irq     # the front panel
